DEEP[22]=("<i>Vedānāṃ sāma-vedo’smi</i>, “of the Vedas I am the Sāmaveda”. <i>Devānām asmi vāsavaḥ</i>, “of the gods, Vāsava”. <i>Indriyāṇāṃ manaś cāsmi</i>, “of the senses, I am the mind”. <i>Bhūtānām asmi cetanā</i>, “in beings, consciousness”.",
 "The Sāmaveda sets the hymns of the Ṛg Veda to melody; it is the root of Indian classical music, and the Chāndogya Upaniṣad (1.1.2) calls the sāman the essence of the ṛc. Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa chooses the musical Veda: devotion loves song. <i>Vāsava</i>, “lord of the Vasus”, is Indra, king of the gods. The mind is called the chief of the senses, since all sense knowledge passes through it; Sāṅkhya calls it the eleventh sense. <i>Cetanā</i>, awareness, is the light by which beings know. Śaṅkara says consciousness is what makes beings alive.",
 "Music, mind and awareness are each a doorway. Listen to something beautiful today with full attention.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Rudrāṇāṃ śaṅkaraś cāsmi</i>, “of the Rudras I am Śaṅkara”. <i>Vitteśo yakṣa-rakṣasām</i>, “of yakṣas and rākṣasas, the lord of wealth”. <i>Vasūnāṃ pāvakaś cāsmi</i>, “of the Vasus I am fire”. <i>Meruḥ śikhariṇām aham</i>, “of peaks, Meru”.",
 "The eleven Rudras are storm deities; Śaṅkara, “the one who brings peace”, is Śiva, their chief. Commentators of different schools read this naturally in their own way. The Smārta tradition, following Śaṅkara the teacher, sees one God in Viṣṇu and Śiva. Vaiṣṇava schools see Śiva as a great god dependent on Viṣṇu. Śaiva traditions see Śiva as supreme, with the Gītā praising him here. The Gītā itself does not settle this, and we do not take sides. Kubera, lord of wealth, rules the yakṣas. The eight Vasus are elemental gods, Agni (fire) among them. Meru is the golden cosmic mountain at the centre of the world in Purāṇic geography.",
 "Different names, one Divine. Respect the many ways people name the sacred.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Purodhasāṃ ca mukhyaṃ māṃ</i>, “of household priests, the chief”. <i>Viddhi pārtha bṛhaspatim</i>, “know me as Bṛhaspati, Pārtha”. <i>Senānīnām ahaṃ skandaḥ</i>, “of generals I am Skanda”. <i>Sarasām asmi sāgaraḥ</i>, “of waters, the ocean”.",
 "Bṛhaspati is the guru and priest of the gods, the planet Jupiter in Indian astronomy; Thursday, <i>guru-vāra</i>, is his day. Skanda, also called Kārttikeya, Murugan and Subrahmaṇya, is the son of Śiva and Pārvatī and the commander of the gods’ army against the demon Tāraka. Commentators note the range: wisdom (Bṛhaspati), courage (Skanda), vastness (the ocean). Arjuna, about to fight under a commander, is reminded that the best general is a glory of the Lord. The ocean appears again as an image of fullness in 2.70.",
 "Excellence in any role, as a teacher, a leader or simply as steady depth, reflects the Divine.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Maharṣīṇāṃ bhṛgur ahaṃ</i>, “of great sages I am Bhṛgu”. <i>Girām asmy ekam akṣaram</i>, “of words, the one syllable”. <i>Yajñānāṃ japa-yajño’smi</i>, “of sacrifices, the sacrifice of japa”. <i>Sthāvarāṇāṃ himālayaḥ</i>, “of the immovable, the Himālaya”.",
 "Bhṛgu is an ancient sage; in a Purāṇic story he kicked Viṣṇu on the chest to test him, and Viṣṇu humbly asked whether his foot was hurt. The mark, <i>śrīvatsa</i>, is said to remain on Viṣṇu’s chest. <i>Ekam akṣaram</i> is Oṃ. Commentators treasure <i>japa-yajña</i>: repeating a sacred name or mantra needs no materials, harms no being, and can be done anywhere. Manusmṛti (2.85–87) ranks japa above other offerings. The Himālaya, <i>abode of snow</i>, is the home of Śiva and many sages.",
 "Repeating a sacred name quietly is a sacrifice anyone can offer, anytime. Try it on a walk.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Aśvatthaḥ sarva-vṛkṣāṇāṃ</i>, “of all trees, the aśvattha”. <i>Devarṣīṇāṃ ca nāradaḥ</i>, “of divine sages, Nārada”. <i>Gandharvāṇāṃ citrarathaḥ</i>, “of gandharvas, Citraratha”. <i>Siddhānāṃ kapilo muniḥ</i>, “of the perfected, the sage Kapila”.",
 "The <i>aśvattha</i>, sacred fig or peepal (<i>Ficus religiosa</i>), is worshipped across India; people circle it and tie threads around it. The Buddha attained awakening under one, the Bodhi tree. Chapter 15 uses the inverted aśvattha as an image of the world. Nārada, the divine sage, wanders singing the Lord’s names and appears in countless stories. Citraratha is a king of the gandharvas, celestial musicians. Kapila, the founder of Sāṅkhya philosophy, is honoured in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa as an avatāra who taught his mother Devahūti. Commentators note the mix: a tree, a singer, a musician, a philosopher.",
 "Look at a peepal tree next time you pass one. Let it remind you of the source of all growth.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Uccaiḥśravasam aśvānāṃ</i>, “of horses, Uccaiḥśravas”. <i>Viddhi mām amṛtodbhavam</i>, “born of nectar, know me”. <i>Airāvataṃ gajendrāṇāṃ</i>, “of lordly elephants, Airāvata”. <i>Narāṇāṃ ca narādhipam</i>, “and of men, the king”.",
 "The churning of the ocean of milk, <i>samudra-manthana</i>, is told in the Bhāgavata and Viṣṇu Purāṇas and the Mahābhārata. Gods and demons churned the ocean with Mount Mandara on Viṣṇu’s tortoise back, using Vāsuki as the rope. Out came fourteen treasures, including Uccaiḥśravas, the white horse; Airāvata, Indra’s white elephant; Lakṣmī; and the nectar of immortality. Commentators note that the just king is named as a glory among humans: good rule is divine. Śaṅkara says the king, protecting dharma, reflects the Lord’s power.",
 "Leadership that protects and serves is a reflection of the Divine. Lead like that wherever you can.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Āyudhānām ahaṃ vajraṃ</i>, “of weapons I am the thunderbolt”. <i>Dhenūnām asmi kāmadhuk</i>, “of cows, the wish-fulfilling cow”. <i>Prajanaś cāsmi kandarpaḥ</i>, “of procreators, Kandarpa”. <i>Sarpāṇām asmi vāsukiḥ</i>, “of serpents, Vāsuki”.",
 "The <i>vajra</i>, Indra’s thunderbolt, was made from the bones of the sage Dadhīci, who gave his life so the gods could defeat the demon Vṛtra. Commentators note the sacrifice behind the weapon. <i>Kāmadhuk</i> or Kāmadhenu, the wish-fulfilling cow, also came from the churning; Vasiṣṭha’s cow Nandinī is her daughter. Kandarpa, Kāma, the god of love, is named as the power of procreation, linking to 7.11: desire within dharma is divine. Vāsuki, king of serpents, served as the churning rope and adorns Śiva’s neck.",
 "Love that creates life is sacred. Honour it with care and respect.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Anantaś cāsmi nāgānāṃ</i>, “of nāgas I am Ananta”. <i>Varuṇo yādasām aham</i>, “of water-dwellers, Varuṇa”. <i>Pitṝṇām aryamā cāsmi</i>, “of ancestors, Aryamā”. <i>Yamaḥ saṃyamatām aham</i>, “of those who govern, Yama”.",
 "Ananta, also called Śeṣa, is the many-headed serpent on whom Viṣṇu reclines on the cosmic ocean; he holds the worlds on his hoods. Balarāma and Lakṣmaṇa are said to be his incarnations. Varuṇa, an ancient Vedic god, rules the waters and guards cosmic order, <i>ṛta</i>. Aryamā is chief of the ancestors. Yama, lord of death, is also <i>Dharmarāja</i>, the king of justice; he taught Naciketas in the Kaṭha Upaniṣad. Commentators note <i>saṃyamatām</i>, “of those who restrain”: death disciplines all. Yudhiṣṭhira was born of Dharma, Yama’s form.",
 "Order and discipline, even the hard discipline of mortality, are part of the sacred pattern.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Prahlādaś cāsmi daityānāṃ</i>, “of the daityas I am Prahlāda”. <i>Kālaḥ kalayatām aham</i>, “of reckoners, I am time”. <i>Mṛgāṇāṃ ca mṛgendro’haṃ</i>, “of beasts, the lion”. <i>Vainateyaś ca pakṣiṇām</i>, “of birds, Vinatā’s son”.",
 "Prahlāda was the son of Hiraṇyakaśipu, a demon king who hated Viṣṇu. The boy loved Viṣṇu from childhood despite his father’s cruelty. He was thrown from cliffs, trampled by elephants and placed in fire, yet unharmed. Finally Viṣṇu appeared as Narasiṃha, half man and half lion, from a pillar, and killed the king. Commentators love this inclusion: devotion is not bound by birth. Time, <i>kāla</i>, reckons all, and returns as the Lord’s terrible form in 11.32. Garuḍa, son of Vinatā, is the eagle who carries Viṣṇu.",
 "Goodness can bloom in the hardest soil. Never assume where it cannot grow.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Pavanaḥ pavatām asmi</i>, “of purifiers I am the wind”. <i>Rāmaḥ śastra-bhṛtām aham</i>, “of weapon-bearers I am Rāma”. <i>Jhaṣāṇāṃ makaraś cāsmi</i>, “of fishes I am the makara”. <i>Srotasām asmi jāhnavī</i>, “of rivers, the Jāhnavī”.",
 "Wind is a purifier: it dries, freshens and carries away. Commentators debate which Rāma is meant. Most, including Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja, take Rāma of Ayodhyā, hero of the Rāmāyaṇa. Some take Paraśurāma, the warrior-sage. The <i>makara</i> is a mythical sea creature, sometimes a crocodile, the mount of Gaṅgā and Varuṇa. Gaṅgā is called Jāhnavī because the sage Jahnu swallowed her when her flood disturbed his rites, then released her from his ear. Gaṅgā descended from heaven through Śiva’s hair at the prayer of Bhagīratha.",
 "Fresh wind and flowing water renew. Step outside and feel them as small blessings.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Sargāṇām ādir antaś ca</i>, “of creations, the beginning and the end”. <i>Madhyaṃ caivāham arjuna</i>, “and the middle am I, Arjuna”. <i>Adhyātma-vidyā vidyānāṃ</i>, “of sciences, the science of the Self”. <i>Vādaḥ pravadatām aham</i>, “of debaters, I am the reasoning”.",
 "<i>Adhyātma-vidyā</i>, the science of the Self, is called the highest knowledge because it leads to freedom; the Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (1.1.4–5) distinguishes higher knowledge, of the Imperishable, from lower, of all other subjects. Indian logic (Nyāya) names three kinds of debate. <i>Vāda</i> is honest discussion to find the truth. <i>Jalpa</i> is debate to win by any means. <i>Vitaṇḍā</i> is mere destructive criticism without a position. Commentators say only <i>vāda</i> is the Lord’s glory. The verse is often quoted to encourage honest dialogue.",
 "In any argument, ask: am I seeking the truth, or only trying to win? Choose the first.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Akṣarāṇām akāro’smi</i>, “of letters I am A”. <i>Dvandvaḥ sāmāsikasya ca</i>, “of compounds, the dvandva”. <i>Aham evākṣayaḥ kālo</i>, “I am imperishable Time”. <i>Dhātāhaṃ viśvato-mukhaḥ</i>, “I am the dispenser, facing all directions”.",
 "In Sanskrit script, every consonant carries an inherent “a”; so “a” is present in nearly all speech. The Aitareya Āraṇyaka (2.3.6) says “a” is all speech. Commentators love this. A <i>dvandva</i> compound joins two or more words as equals, like <i>rāma-lakṣmaṇau</i>, “Rāma and Lakṣmaṇa”. It is the most balanced compound, neither word ruling the other. <i>Akṣaya kāla</i>, undecaying time, is time beyond all measured time. <i>Viśvato-mukha</i>, facing every way, means the Lord sees and gives to all beings at once.",
 "The first sound and the first step hold everything that follows. Honour beginnings.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Mṛtyuḥ sarva-haraś cāham</i>, “I am all-devouring death”. <i>Udbhavaś ca bhaviṣyatām</i>, “and the origin of all that will be”. <i>Kīrtiḥ śrīr vāk ca nārīṇāṃ</i>, “among the feminine, fame, fortune, speech”. <i>Smṛtir medhā dhṛtiḥ kṣamā</i>, “memory, intelligence, steadfastness, forgiveness”.",
 "Seven qualities named in Sanskrit’s feminine gender, often personified as goddesses. <i>Kīrti</i>, fame; <i>śrī</i>, fortune or beauty, Lakṣmī; <i>vāk</i>, speech, Sarasvatī; <i>smṛti</i>, memory; <i>medhā</i>, intelligence; <i>dhṛti</i>, steadfastness; <i>kṣamā</i>, forgiveness. Commentators note that some read <i>nārīṇām</i> as “among women”, praising these qualities in women, and others as “among feminine words”. The list ends with forgiveness, the gentlest strength. The pairing with death and birth in the first half shows the Lord as both end and beginning.",
 "Memory, steadiness and forgiveness: cultivate them as divine gifts within you.")
DEEP[35]=("<i>Bṛhat-sāma tathā sāmnāṃ</i>, “of Sāma hymns, the Bṛhat-sāma”. <i>Gāyatrī chandasām aham</i>, “of metres, the Gāyatrī”. <i>Māsānāṃ mārgaśīrṣo’ham</i>, “of months, Mārgaśīrṣa”. <i>Ṛtūnāṃ kusumākaraḥ</i>, “of seasons, the flowering spring”.",
 "The Bṛhat-sāma is a great chant used in the Soma sacrifice, addressed to Indra. The Gāyatrī metre has three lines of eight syllables; the famous Gāyatrī mantra (Ṛg Veda 3.62.10) is in this metre. Mārgaśīrṣa (November–December) was once counted the first month of the year; Gītā Jayantī falls on its bright ekādaśī, celebrating the day the Gītā was spoken. The Bhāgavata says the gopīs began their vow for Kṛṣṇa in this month. Spring, <i>vasanta</i>, is <i>kusumākara</i>, “the mine of flowers”.",
 "This very season, Mārgaśīrṣa, is named by Kṛṣṇa as his own. Let it be a time to read and reflect.")
DEEP[36]=("<i>Dyūtaṃ chalayatām asmi</i>, “I am the gambling of cheats”. <i>Tejas tejasvinām aham</i>, “the splendour of the splendid”. <i>Jayo’smi vyavasāyo’smi</i>, “I am victory, I am effort”. <i>Sattvaṃ sattvavatām aham</i>, “the goodness of the good”.",
 "This surprising line has puzzled readers. Commentators explain that in each class Kṛṣṇa names the most powerful; among deceivers, gambling is the most powerful deceit. Śaṅkara says the verse shows the Lord’s presence even in power used wrongly; it is not approval. Rāmānuja says the Lord gives each being the power it uses, and they choose how to use it. The irony is sharp: the Pāṇḍavas lost everything in a rigged dice game, and here, before the war that game caused, Kṛṣṇa names gambling among his glories. Effort and victory follow, and goodness closes the line.",
 "Power itself is neutral. Whether it becomes good or harm depends on how we use it.")
DEEP[37]=("<i>Vṛṣṇīnāṃ vāsudevo’smi</i>, “of the Vṛṣṇis I am Vāsudeva”. <i>Pāṇḍavānāṃ dhanañjayaḥ</i>, “of the Pāṇḍavas, Dhanañjaya”. <i>Munīnām apy ahaṃ vyāsaḥ</i>, “of sages I am Vyāsa”. <i>Kavīnām uśanā kaviḥ</i>, “of poets, the poet Uśanā”.",
 "Kṛṣṇa names himself (Vāsudeva, of the Vṛṣṇi clan) and then Arjuna (Dhanañjaya) among his glories. Commentators find this tender: Arjuna hears that he himself is a glory of God. Vyāsa, compiler of the Vedas and author of the Mahābhārata, is named; the Gītā’s own author appears in his text. Uśanā is Śukrācārya, the teacher of the asuras, famous for wisdom and policy; he knew the secret of reviving the dead. Rāmānuja says naming Arjuna shows the Lord’s love. The list shows the Lord in his own family, his friend, his poet.",
 "You too can be a glory of the Divine, by living at your best. That is not pride; it is purpose.")
DEEP[38]=("<i>Daṇḍo damayatām asmi</i>, “I am the rod of those who punish”. <i>Nītir asmi jigīṣatām</i>, “the policy of those who seek victory”. <i>Maunaṃ caivāsmi guhyānāṃ</i>, “of secrets I am silence”. <i>Jñānaṃ jñānavatām aham</i>, “the knowledge of the knowers”.",
 "<i>Daṇḍa</i>, the rod, is the power of just punishment that keeps order; the law-books say that without it, the strong would devour the weak like fish. <i>Nīti</i>, policy or right strategy, is the art of statecraft. Commentators note the subtle phrase <i>maunaṃ guhyānām</i>: the best way to keep a secret is silence, and the deepest truth is known in silence. Śaṅkara says the knowledge of the Self is the highest secret, known in inner stillness. Many traditions prize silence: Dakṣiṇāmūrti, Śiva as teacher, is said to teach in silence.",
 "Silence protects what is precious. Spend a few minutes in it each day.")
DEEP[39]=("<i>Yac cāpi sarva-bhūtānāṃ</i>, “and whatever, of all beings”. <i>Bījaṃ tad aham arjuna</i>, “is the seed, that am I, Arjuna”. <i>Na tad asti vinā yat syān</i>, “there is nothing that could exist without”. <i>Mayā bhūtaṃ carācaram</i>, “me, moving or unmoving”.",
 "After naming the best in each class, Kṛṣṇa states the general rule: not only the best, but everything, depends on him. Commentators recall 7.10 and 9.18, where the Lord is called the seed. Śaṅkara says the seed is the cause from which all arise. Rāmānuja says nothing has being without the Lord as its inner support. The verse turns the list from a catalogue of wonders into a teaching about dependence. The excellent shows the Lord clearly; the ordinary shows him too, if one looks.",
 "Everything you meet today depends on the same source. Let that make you a little more reverent.")
DEEP[40]=("<i>Nānto’sti mama divyānāṃ</i>, “there is no end to my divine”. <i>Vibhūtīnāṃ parantapa</i>, “glories, scorcher of foes”. <i>Eṣa tūddeśataḥ prokto</i>, “this has been told briefly”. <i>Vibhūter vistaro mayā</i>, “an account of my glory, by me”.",
 "<i>Uddeśataḥ</i>, “by way of example”. Commentators say the list was never meant to be complete. Its purpose was to train the eye. Śaṅkara says the Lord’s glories are infinite, so only samples can be given. Rāmānuja says the list shows enough for meditation. Many later texts expand the idea. The Viṣṇu Sahasranāma gives a thousand names; Śiva and Devī have their own thousand-name hymns. Each tradition finds the Divine in countless forms.",
 "The Divine is more than any list can hold. Keep adding your own examples as you notice them.")
DEEP[41]=("<i>Yad yad vibhūtimat sattvaṃ</i>, “whatever being is glorious”. <i>Śrīmad ūrjitam eva vā</i>, “prosperous or powerful”. <i>Tat tad evāvagaccha tvaṃ</i>, “know that indeed”. <i>Mama tejo’ṃśa-sambhavam</i>, “as born of a spark of my splendour”.",
 "This verse is the method of the whole chapter in one line. Commentators say it turns the world into a temple: great music, a brilliant mind, a mountain, an act of courage, a kind heart, each is a spark. Śaṅkara says it gives the seeker a way to meditate on the Lord in all excellence. Rāmānuja says it shows that all glory belongs to the Lord, not to its bearer; so admiration need not become envy or idolatry. Tagore and Aurobindo, among others, found in this verse a spiritual basis for art and science.",
 "When you admire something excellent, pause and say inwardly: a spark of the Divine. It turns admiration into worship.")
DEEP[42]=("<i>Atha vā bahunaitena</i>, “but what need of so much”. <i>Kiṃ jñātena tavārjuna</i>, “knowing this, Arjuna”. <i>Viṣṭabhyāham idaṃ kṛtsnam</i>, “I stand, supporting this whole”. <i>Ekāṃśena sthito jagat</i>, “world, with one fragment”.",
 "The Puruṣa Sūkta (Ṛg Veda 10.90.3) says: <i>pādo’sya viśvā bhūtāni, tripād asyāmṛtaṃ divi</i>, “all beings are one quarter of him; three quarters are immortal in heaven”. Commentators connect the two verses. Śaṅkara says the whole universe is only a small part of the Lord. Rāmānuja says the Lord supports the universe with a fraction of his power. The verse prepares for chapter 11: Arjuna, having heard of the glories, will ask to see them. The colophon names chapter 10 Vibhūti Yoga.",
 "However vast the universe, the Divine is vaster still. Let that make your troubles feel smaller.")
